{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner N1 direction, 2026-10-04, after a complete read of notes/2026-10-03-proposal-artifact-reads.md at 41b304e7c2d997aec352f01b8c11b61ff983010e (631 lines, 32,558 bytes), the complete separate provenance note and four-path inventory. Original 53016b8e/4ba647c2 remains the sole design/specification and implementing-lane handoff. I independently matched both copied 006 files byte for byte against the frozen shared files: map 24,013 bytes SHA2567bdca0fd19a8c26613c9e9ea427c6cea1c3296c7fa429ff3ff1561135e03a381; ledger 9,143 bytes SHA256763afb79e879ae59da2f0096108b894ab3a168e31ec274ab09af14dc0cb28fdd. Preserve those bytes and separate provenance; do not copy dirty root README or frozen 005–016 opportunistically. No adoption follows from copying them.\n\nFive proposed defaults for the revised design: (1) Use the current authenticated member-backed read path, including valid delegated read sessions, without requiring the reader to hold a lane or qualify for a specific obligation. This is a proposed new artifact-disclosure decision, not already-existing file authority: explicitly state that members and valid delegates can read the complete permitted trees/history, including unchanged files and committed secrets. Narrower per-policy reads remain a separate unresolved design, not silently delivered. C1 direct owner/admin private reads and separately signed controls remain excluded. (2) Serve immutable inputs and compute presentation diffs in a shared public client module, subject to the completeness repairs below; hunks must not become admission evidence. (3) Keep 1 MiB content and 200-commit pages as proposed browser limits, with deterministic response/work limits and the smaller future MCP presentation budget explicitly reconciled below. (4) Retain the proposed four commit fields, leaving personal author/date fields out; raw headers remain unverified and bounded, never silently truncated into a different change identity. (5) Amendment/section numbers are provisional and checked at composition. These are recommendations for the next exact design, not protocol adoption or implementing-lane commissions.\n\nA. Fix the comparison-base contract before calling it exact. R-ART-5's 'base equals head means empty tree' is false for an ordinary unchanged proposal on nonempty main. Source at d691: ArtifactsAdapter.diff(main===null) uses EMPTY_TREE while storing base=head; with main===head, changedPaths instead compares that actual tree with itself and the stored base also equals head. More importantly changedPaths handles unrelated histories with EMPTY_TREE and no bases, while ArtifactsAdapter.diff stores fallback main; that stored base need not be reachable from the pinned head. Multiple merge bases produce a union of changed paths, with the adapter recording one sorted base. Sections 2/4/7 must not assume every stored base is the one exact display baseline and reachable from the head. Specify the retained comparison identity needed for empty, ordinary, unrelated and multiple-base cases, its relation to recorded changed paths and the exact generation commit set. Explain how retained generations are handled using available immutable facts; do not guess from equality or today's main, erase old semantics, silently narrow admission, or claim objects are retained under a ref that does not reach them. Include an honest unavailable/qualified display where old facts cannot support the claim, and name any actual additional retention or contract decision in this N1 design. These are N1 design gaps, not a new finding against the pending verifier release.\n\nB. Define the commit range, ordering and permitted history inputs. 'From base to head, oldest first' is incomplete for merges, roots, unrelated histories and criss-cross history. Define the DAG set, parent-before-child order with deterministic tie breaks, complete work bound and stable cursor bound to room/lane/generation/head and query. Page limits do not bound the graph walk by themselves. The current UI changeHistory input has one required parent and its patch() reads the commit and that parent's tree; the proposed API lists parents but only permits files at base/head/listed commits. A listed side-branch commit can have a parent in the excluded base ancestry that is not the one base, and a root has no parent. State how the shared comparison obtains those authorized parent snapshots or visibly reports unsupported history, and how merge parents are selected. Do not claim the existing changes.ts can consume the three methods unchanged: it currently needs CommitStore.readCommit/readTree/readBlob. Show the bounded public adapter or explicit snapshot representation and the root/merge handling. Preserve duplicate/headerless IDs and ambiguous mapped hunks as qualified presentation, never exact review/carry evidence. This does not commission arbitrary object-ID reads or another universal history API.\n\nC. Complete the resource bounds and failure grammar. Raw changeId, parents arrays, link targets, path inputs, tree fan-out, cached commit lists and total encoded responses need limits alongside file bytes. A symlink target is content, contrary to the blanket 'link has no content' sentence and R-ART-5; either return a bounded inert target explicitly or omit it consistently. Define missing retained commit/tree/blob and upstream errors as unavailable/error, never an absent file or an empty diff. Bound caches by count/bytes and keep mutable latest/authority separate from cached immutable bytes. The inspected read route and Room.read at d691 do not show general read throttling; the existing rateLimit calls cover redemption, so remove the claim that 'the Room's existing throttling' already protects these reads. If a new bounded read work/admission control is necessary, describe it as proposed with an owner, not as shipped or an extra first-Jam prerequisite. Exact sizes and one compact countercase can be settled in the implementation review; no per-field sweep is requested.\n\nD. Make pagination and returned identities coherent. Every result should carry the resolved generation/head and the actual comparison/commit identities needed by its consumer; the file type currently omits latest, while acceptance says every old-generation read reports the newer generation. Either include that advisory field with its sampling time, or limit the stated guarantee accurately and use the existing proposal/lane read. Define since equal/later/unknown generations, malformed IDs/path/page limits and cross-generation cursor use, with HTTPS/RPC error parity and one explicit not-found form. Identity mismatches and unavailable content must remain distinguishable. A cache hit still checks current read authority. State the authorization point for a read that awaits pins/provider data; preserve the promised revocation behavior with a deliberate final check or explicitly qualified in-flight semantics, not a claim that no await/race exists.\n\nE. Reconcile the presentation and transport owners. A whole 1 MiB file, 200 metadata records and an unpaginated list of up to 100,000 changed paths cannot be advertised as directly satisfying the MCP plan's 20 KB text/slice budget. Define the bounded core page/slice contract or an explicit MCP-owned bounded presentation adapter over these identities, including complete/partial/too-large signaling and a cursor where applicable. Keep all adopted MCP diff/file obligations, including its separately planned main-file access, with the existing MCP/N1 owners; the latter is not ownerless merely because N1's first proposal API excludes it. Browser and cold reviewer must see what could not be compared and avoid calling an incomplete diff unchanged or ready for review. No need to make the Room compute hunks if the public input route can fulfill that result.\n\nF. Correct the source and scope labels. The new all-member file exposure is real: current metadata/log access and a checker job token do not prove every member already has the file bytes. Revocation stops subsequent authorized reads and cannot recall received bytes; the future private C1 access is not 'on their device' only, but a trusted hosted direct-device session path. R-PROP-2 already specifies pinned-ref retention while the Room exists, so it is wrong to call the duration of object-pinning refs wholly unspecified; distinguish future end-of-room/orphan cleanup and fork retention, with existing C1/C3/workspace and cleanup owners. Fix the introductory sources link from section 13 to section 16, and align too-large/file-too-large terminology and symlink statements.\n\nRetain the full original reader/operation matrix, concrete allowed/refused and moved-generation examples, source/export authority, private boundary and review without fork write credentials; the cold no-transcript immutable review, bound line question, revision, here/carried/rejected/stale evidence and publication watermark; and all implementing lanes with their explicit dependencies. Revise the compact acceptance to prove the repaired boundaries (not merely each type row), with empty versus unchanged/nonempty comparison, history-parent/root/merge limits, incomplete content and revoked/cached access covered economically. Independent exact source design review with Architecture/Security/Simplification and adoption still precedes a separate implementing commission. Original C5/C6 acceptance, all MCP scope, the 10× test priority and builder's first-useful-Jam readiness remain unchanged. No runtime test, install, probe, provider action, source edit or root commit was performed.","body":{"head":"41b304e7c2d997aec352f01b8c11b61ff983010e","no_git_artifact":"true","scope":"N1 planning defaults and source-grounded design corrections only; independent review/adoption before implementation.","source_approval":"none"}}